.TH STTY 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
stty \- set or report terminal I/O characteristics
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stty
.RB [ \-a | \-g ]
.br
.B stty
.I operand
.RI [ operand ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B stty
sets, or reports, the I/O characteristics of the terminal open as
standard input. There is no operand naming a different device;
standard input is always the terminal affected.
.PP
With no operands at all, a subset of the current settings is reported:
the line speed, every special control character whose value differs
from a fixed set of conventional defaults, and every mode operand
whose current state differs from that same conventional default set.
.B \-a
reports the same three groups in full, not only the entries that
differ from the defaults.
.B \-g
reports the complete current settings as a single, machine\-readable
string: 22 colon\-separated hexadecimal fields (the input, output,
control, and local mode words, the sixteen special control character
values, and the input and output speeds), chosen as one shell word
requiring no further quoting. That same string, given back as the
.I sole
operand of a later invocation, is recognized and applied directly (see
.B "Combination Modes"
below); combined with any other operand, it is rejected as an
invalid argument.
.B \-a
and
.B \-g
may not be combined with each other, or with any other operand.
.PP
Every operand below is parsed before anything is actually changed: an
invocation fails, and applies none of its operands at all, on the
first operand it does not recognize or that has an invalid value.
Among a set of operands that are otherwise all valid, a later operand
always wins over an earlier, conflicting one for the same setting
(e.g. in
.RB \` "stty echo \-echo" \' ,
.B \-echo
wins); this includes a bare speed number and an explicit
.BR ispeed / ospeed
given together, where the explicit
.BR ispeed / ospeed
always takes precedence for its own direction over the bare number,
regardless of the order they appear in.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Report all settings, not only those differing from the defaults.
Mutually exclusive with every other operand, including
.BR \-g .
.TP
.B \-g
Report all settings as a single, re\-usable, colon\-separated
hexadecimal string. Mutually exclusive with every other operand,
including
.BR \-a .
.SH OPERANDS
Every mode operand below may be prefixed with
.B \-
to select the opposite (disabled) state, except where noted. A
platform's own terminal driver determines whether a given Control Mode
actually takes effect once set; this implementation always accepts and
stores every value below, and reports back exactly what is currently
stored.
.SS Control Modes
.TP
.BR parenb ", " \-parenb
Enable, or disable, parity generation and detection.
.TP
.BR parodd ", " \-parodd
Select odd, or even, parity, when
.B parenb
is enabled.
.TP
.BR cs5 ", " cs6 ", " cs7 ", " cs8
Select 5, 6, 7, or 8 bits per character. These four are mutually
exclusive; whichever appears last in the operand list wins.
.TP
.BR hupcl ", " \-hupcl
Hang up (or do not hang up) the line when the last process with the
terminal open closes it.
.TP
.BR cstopb ", " \-cstopb
Use two, or one, stop bits per character.
.TP
.BR cread ", " \-cread
Enable, or disable, the receiver.
.TP
.BR clocal ", " \-clocal
Assume, or do not assume, a connection with no need to monitor a
carrier signal.
.TP
.I number
A plain decimal number, on its own, sets both the input and output
speed to that value.
.TP
.BI ispeed " number"
Set only the input speed.
.TP
.BI ospeed " number"
Set only the output speed.
.SS Input Modes
.TP
.BR ignbrk ", " \-ignbrk
Ignore, or do not ignore, break characters.
.TP
.BR brkint ", " \-brkint
Signal, or do not signal,
.B INTR
on break.
.TP
.BR ignpar ", " \-ignpar
Ignore, or do not ignore, characters with parity errors.
.TP
.BR parmrk ", " \-parmrk
Mark, or do not mark, parity errors.
.TP
.BR inpck ", " \-inpck
Enable, or disable, input parity checking.
.TP
.BR istrip ", " \-istrip
Strip, or do not strip, input characters to seven bits.
.TP
.BR inlcr ", " \-inlcr
Translate, or do not translate, newline to carriage return on input.
.TP
.BR igncr ", " \-igncr
Ignore, or do not ignore, carriage return on input.
.TP
.BR icrnl ", " \-icrnl
Translate, or do not translate, carriage return to newline on input.
.TP
.BR ixon ", " \-ixon
Enable, or disable, start/stop output control.
.TP
.BR ixany ", " \-ixany
Allow, or do not allow, any character to restart output (not just the
.B START
character).
.TP
.BR ixoff ", " \-ixoff
Enable, or disable, start/stop input control.
.SS Output Modes
.TP
.BR opost ", " \-opost
Enable, or disable, output processing (post\-processing of output
characters).
.TP
.BR onlcr ", " \-onlcr
Translate, or do not translate, newline to carriage\-return/newline on
output.
.TP
.BR ocrnl ", " \-ocrnl
Translate, or do not translate, carriage return to newline on output.
.TP
.BR onocr ", " \-onocr
Do not, or do, output carriage returns at column zero.
.TP
.BR onlret ", " \-onlret
Assume, or do not assume, that newline performs the carriage\-return
function.
.TP
.BR ofill ", " \-ofill
Use fill characters, or timing, for output delays.
.TP
.BR ofdel ", " \-ofdel
Use
.B DEL,
or
.BR NUL ,
as fill characters, when
.B ofill
is enabled.
.TP
.BR cr0 ", " cr1 ", " cr2 ", " cr3
Select the carriage\-return delay style.
.TP
.BR nl0 ", " nl1
Select the newline delay style.
.TP
.BR tab0 ", " tab1 ", " tab2 ", " tab3 ", " tabs ", " \-tabs
Select the horizontal\-tab delay style.
.B tabs
is a synonym for
.BR tab3 ;
.B \-tabs
is a synonym for
.BR tab0 .
.TP
.BR bs0 ", " bs1
Select the backspace delay style.
.TP
.BR ff0 ", " ff1
Select the form\-feed delay style.
.TP
.BR vt0 ", " vt1
Select the vertical\-tab delay style.
.SS Local Modes
.TP
.BR isig ", " \-isig
Enable, or disable, the
.BR INTR ", " QUIT ", and " SUSP
special characters.
.TP
.BR icanon ", " \-icanon
Enable, or disable, canonical (line\-buffered, editable) input.
.TP
.BR iexten ", " \-iexten
Enable, or disable, implementation\-defined input character
processing.
.TP
.BR echo ", " \-echo
Echo back, or do not echo back, input characters.
.TP
.BR echoe ", " \-echoe
If
.B icanon
is set, visually erase, or do not visually erase, a character on
.BR ERASE .
.TP
.BR echok ", " \-echok
If
.B icanon
is set, echo, or do not echo, a newline after
.BR KILL .
.TP
.BR echonl ", " \-echonl
If
.B icanon
is set, echo, or do not echo, a newline even when
.B echo
is disabled.
.TP
.BR noflsh ", " \-noflsh
Disable, or enable, flushing after the
.BR INTR ", " QUIT ", and " SUSP
special characters.
.TP
.BR tostop ", " \-tostop
Send, or do not send,
.B SIGTTOU
to a background process attempting to write to the terminal.
.SS Special Control Character Assignments
.TP
.BI intr " character"
.TP
.BI quit " character"
.TP
.BI erase " character"
.TP
.BI kill " character"
.TP
.BI eof " character"
.TP
.BI eol " character"
.TP
.BI start " character"
.TP
.BI stop " character"
.TP
.BI susp " character"
Set the named special control character. Each
.I character
is one of: a single literal character; a
\`^\fIX\fR\'
pair, where
.I X
is a letter (matched case\-insensitively against the conventional
control\-character encoding, e.g.
.RB \` ^C \'
for
.BR INTR 's
usual value) or one of
.RB \` [ \' ", " \` \e \' ", " \` ] \' ", " \` ^ \' ", " \` _ \' ,
or
.RB \` ? \' ;
or
.RB \` ^\- \'
or
.RB \` undef \'
to disable that character entirely.
.TP
.BI min " number"
.TP
.BI time " number"
Set the
.B MIN
or
.B TIME
value used in non\-canonical input processing, an integer from 0 to
255.
.TP
.B ek
Reset the
.B ERASE
and
.B KILL
characters to their conventional default values, leaving every other
setting untouched.
.SS Combination Modes
.TP
.BR evenp ", " parity
Equivalent to
.BR "parenb cs7 \-parodd" .
.TP
.B oddp
Equivalent to
.BR "parenb cs7 parodd" .
.TP
.BR \-parity ", " \-evenp ", " \-oddp
Equivalent to
.BR "\-parenb cs8" .
.TP
.B raw
Set character size to 8 bits; disable the
.BR ERASE ", " KILL ", " INTR ", " QUIT ", " EOF ", and " EOL
special characters (as if each were set to
.BR undef ) ;
and disable output processing
.RB ( opost )
and input parity checking
.RB ( inpck ) .
.TP
.BR \-raw ", " cooked
Re\-enable output processing and input parity checking. This is a
narrow, one\-way inverse of
.B raw
that touches only those same two settings; it does not attempt to
restore the character size or the six special characters
.B raw
disables, since their prior values were never recorded.
.TP
.B nl
Equivalent to
.BR \-icrnl .
.TP
.B \-nl
Equivalent to
.BR "icrnl \-inlcr \-igncr" .
.TP
.B sane
Reset every Control/Input/Output/Local Mode operand and delay style
listed above, and every special control character, to a fixed set of
conventional default values \(em the same defaults a freshly
initialized terminal in this implementation starts with.
.TP
.I saved\-settings
A string of exactly 22 colon\-separated hexadecimal fields, in the
same form
.B \-g
prints, given as the
.I only
operand of the invocation. The terminal is set to exactly the state
the string encodes. This form is not recognized, and is instead
treated as an ordinary, unrecognized (and therefore invalid) operand,
if given together with any other operand.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
Every operand mandated by the POSIX specification for
.BR stty (1p)
is implemented, including the Output Modes group, which the
specification marks as an XSI extension. Not implemented: any
BSD\-only or GNU\-only mode operand that has no corresponding standard
operand name (for example
.BR crtscts ) ;
these are refused as invalid operands rather than silently accepted
and ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
.B \-a
or
.B \-g
was combined with another operand, an operand was not recognized, an
operand's value was missing or invalid, or standard input is not a
terminal (or another failure occurred reading or writing its
characteristics).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tty (1),
.BR tput (1)
